        "reason-2": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "2",
            "title": "The study that changed how researchers look at plastic bottles",
            "body": "<p>In 2020, researchers at Trinity College Dublin published a peer-reviewed study in <em>Nature Food</em> testing polypropylene baby bottles — the plastic used in roughly 82% of bottles sold worldwide — under the WHO's own recommended sterilizing-and-mixing steps.</p><p>The result: bottles released as much as 16.2 million microplastic particles per liter, with release rising sharply as water temperature climbed toward boiling. The same team estimated that some bottle-fed infants could be exposed to more than a million particles a day.</p>"
          }
        },
        "image-3": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0979/5082/6776/files/ChatGPT_Image_Jul_13_2026_03_47_16_PM_800x800.png?v=1783932697",
            "alt": "Bottle sterilizing and scratched plastic placeholder",
            "caption": "Macro view of scratches inside a repeatedly washed glass bottle"
          }
        },
        "reason-3": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "3",
            "title": "\"BPA-free\" was never a plastic-free promise",
            "body": "<p>\"BPA-free\" means exactly one thing: the bottle doesn't contain Bisphenol A. It says nothing about whether the plastic sheds particles when heated.</p><p>In 2024, parents filed class-action lawsuits against several major baby bottle makers — including the companies behind Philips Avent, Dr. Brown's, Nuk, and Tommee Tippee — alleging they never warned customers that heating their \"BPA-free\" polypropylene bottles could release microplastics. Courts have allowed some of these failure-to-warn claims to proceed. The label was never the full picture.</p>"
          }
        },
